{"id":"taskflow-case-14-stale-dispatch-and-recovery","display_id":null,"title":"CASE-14 stale dispatch and recovery","description":"Verify stale dispatch receipt, stale continuation binding, closed task continuation, and missing receipt. Recovery/status/taskflow surfaces must give one blocker truth and no impossible commands.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"1779189387048968900","created_by":"vida task","updated_at":"1779313436142760000","closed_at":"1779312476296254600","close_reason":"current-binary-rungraph-closure-complete-after-dispatch-timeout-window-patch-and-exception-takeover-recovery","source_repo":"C:\\project\\vida-stack","compaction_level":0,"original_size":0,"notes":"# CASE-14 Audit Findings — Validation & Correction Required\n\n**Audit Date:** 2026-05-21  \n**Scope:** Stale dispatch receipt, continuation binding gaps, recovery state machines, dead code paths  \n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Critical Defects (Need Fix Before Case Validation)\n\n### D1: Stale State Detection Never Clears — Indefinite False Positives\n**File:** `taskflow_run_graph.rs:1302-1348`  \n**Function:** `projection_stale_state_suspected()`  \n\nThe stale flag is set per-projection call and never cleared once true. If a stale result file persists (e.g., from crashed dispatch), every subsequent projection reports `stale_state_suspected: true` indefinitely — even after manual state repair or intervention.\n\n**Impact:** Run-graph status surfaces will always show stale state for any run that had one crashed dispatch, blocking all downstream operations.\n\n### D2: External Dispatch Stale Detection False Positive\n**File:** `taskflow_run_graph.rs:415-460`  \n**Function:** `blocked_external_dispatch_artifact_mismatched_as_internal_activation()`  \n\nReturns true when receipt has `dispatch_status == \"blocked\"`, `blocker_code == \"internal_activation_view_only\"`, and result file matches, BUT does NOT check if the backend was explicitly configured as external (e.g., hermes_cli, opencode_cli). Legitimate external-backend dispatches that fail with activation-view-only are incorrectly flagged as stale.\n\n### D6: Continuation Binding Blocker Code Missing Run ID Context\n**File:** `taskflow_consume_resume.rs:225-230`  \n**Function:** `consume_continue_resume_error_blocker_code()`  \n\nMaps errors to `\"continuation_binding_ambiguous\"` but does NOT include run_id or task_id. Makes it impossible for automated recovery logic to distinguish which run has ambiguous bindings when multiple runs exist in the same state directory.\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Medium Defects (Should Fix Before Case Validation)\n\n### D3: Continuation Binding Sync Does Not Validate Active Bounded Unit Parity\n**File:** `taskflow_continuation.rs:185-210`  \nAfter sync write, no validation that written binding fields match what was persisted. Race between sync calls could leave stale bounded unit data.\n\n### D4: Missing Receipt Recovery Creates Synthetic Receipt With Incomplete Fields\n**File:** `taskflow_consume_resume.rs:3180-3265`  \n`recover_missing_first_dispatch_receipt()` only populates fields relevant to active lane. `supersedes_receipt_id`, `exception_path_receipt_id`, and downstream dispatch metadata left as defaults — can cause downstream resume logic to misclassify lineage state.\n\n### D5: Stale In-Flight Dispatch Timeout Uses Hardcoded 10s Default\n**File:** `taskflow_consume_resume.rs:3624-3637`  \nFalls back to `STALE_PROJECTION_DISPATCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 10s`. Too aggressive for long-running external backend dispatches (model API calls can take minutes).\n\n### D8: Blocker Code Normalization Loses Context\n**File:** `taskflow_run_graph.rs:95-120`  \nWhen result is empty and blocked evidence exists, falls back to generic `\"ToolExecutionFailed\"` — loses all specific context about what went wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🔵 Dead Code Identified\n\n### DC1: hermes_cli / opencode_cli Test Stubs\n**Files:** `runtime_dispatch_execution.rs (tests)`, `runtime_assignment_builder.rs (tests)`  \nMock backend IDs that don't correspond to any real CLI binary or configured system entry. Only present in test fixtures, never exercised end-to-end with real external backends.\n\n### DC2: `internal_codex_external_fallback_backend()` Fallback Chain Unreachable\n**File:** `runtime_dispatch_execution.rs:1430-1480`  \nFallback path (qwen_cli → ready check) has never been tested with a real external backend failure scenario — tests always use hermes_cli as blocked backend.\n\n### DC3: Placeholder Detection Dead Code\n**File:** `taskflow_run_graph.rs:560-575`  \n`sanitize_placeholder_continuation_bind_command()` checks for `<task-id>` and `<run-id>` placeholders, but no code path generates commands with these literal strings — only present in CLI help text.\n\n### DC4: Legacy Missing First Receipt Repair Unreachable\n**File:** `taskflow_consume_resume.rs:3150-3180`  \nOnly triggered when bootstrap survives but receipt doesn't — in practice, if bootstrap data exists, status should already have correct next_node from bootstrap, making this repair function unreachable.\n\n---\n\n## 🔶 Inconsistencies\n\n### I1: Stale Timeout Values Mismatch\n`STALE_PROJECTION_DISPATCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 10s` used for both projection stale check AND in-flight dispatch stale check. External backend dispatches can legitimately run for minutes — 10s is too aggressive.\n\n### I3: Blocker Code Canonicalization Double-Normalize\n`normalize_run_graph_blocker_codes()` called in multiple code paths (projection truth, recovery contract, status surface), each time potentially deduplicating and transforming the same codes differently — different surfaces report different normalized blocker_code lists for same underlying state.\n\n### I5: Exception Takeover Receipt Matching Overly Permissive\nChecks that receipt has both `exception_path_receipt_id` and `supersedes_receipt_id` non-empty, but does NOT verify these IDs correspond to actual recorded receipts in the state store. Malformed or manually-edited receipt file would pass check without real exception-takeover evidence.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Search Criteria for Validation\n\n```bash\n# Stale detection validation\ngrep -rn \"stale_state_suspected\\|projection_stale_state_suspected\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_run_graph.rs | head -30\n\n# Continuation binding parity  \ngrep -rn \"continuation_binding_matches_reconciled_status\\|effective_projection_continuation_binding\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_run_graph.rs\ngrep -rn \"sync_run_graph_continuation_binding\\|explicit_task_graph_bound_task_id\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_continuation.rs\n\n# Dispatch receipt lifecycle\ngrep -rn \"recover_missing_first_dispatch_receipt\\|dispatch_receipt_retry_eligible\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_consume_resume.rs\ngrep -rn \"normalize_run_graph_blocker_codes\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_run_graph.rs\n\n# Backend references (dead code)\ngrep -rn '\"hermes_cli\"\\|\"opencode_cli\"' crates/vida/src/runtime_dispatch_execution.rs | grep -v test\n\n# Exception takeover validation\ngrep -rn \"active_exception_takeover_receipt_matches_status\\|status_derived_exception_takeover_binding\" crates/vida/src/taskflow_run_graph.rs\n```\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Recommended Fixes (Priority Order)\n\n1. **F1:** Add stale result file expiration + supersede check — prevents indefinite false positives\n2. **F2:** Fix external dispatch stale detection to check backend class before flagging stale\n3. **F6:** Include run_id in continuation binding blocker codes for automated recovery\n4. **F4:** Populate full receipt fields (supersedes, exception) in missing receipt recovery\n5. **F5:** Make stale timeout configurable per backend class (internal=10s, external_cli=300s)\n6. **F7:** Validate exception takeover receipt IDs against state store before accepting as valid\n7. **F8:** Centralize blocker code normalization to single entry point per command surface\n8. **F9-F10:** Add integration test for stale dispatch recovery; remove dead backend test fixtures\n\n---\n\n**Validation Gate:** Before validating CASE-14, fix D1 (stale state indefinite) and D2 (false positive on external backends). These are the root causes of the `continuation_binding_ambiguous` blocker preventing next bounded unit binding.\n","labels":["active-exception-takeover","blocked-tool-execution-failed","case-14","coach-dispatch-configured-backend-failed","dispatch-init-timeout-window-patched","recovery","stale-dispatch","taskflow-testing"],"execution_semantics":{"execution_mode":"sequential","order_bucket":"adversarial-readiness","parallel_group":null,"conflict_domain":"taskflow-release-readiness"},"planner_metadata":{"owned_paths":["crates/vida/src/runtime_dispatch_execution.rs","crates/vida/src/taskflow_consume_resume.rs","crates/vida/src/taskflow_run_graph.rs","crates/vida/tests"],"acceptance_targets":[],"proof_targets":["lane-show-coach-tool-execution-failed-evidence","recovery-latest-open-delegated-cycle"],"risk":null,"estimate":null,"lane_hint":null},"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"taskflow-case-14-stale-dispatch-and-recovery","depends_on_id":"taskflow-case-13-parallel-fanout-and-conflict-domain","edge_type":"blocks","created_at":"1779189501350712100","created_by":"vida","metadata":"{}","thread_id":""},{"issue_id":"taskflow-case-14-stale-dispatch-and-recovery","depends_on_id":"taskflow-adversarial-autonomy-and-release-readiness-testing-epic","edge_type":"parent-child","created_at":"1779189387048968900","created_by":"vida task","metadata":"{}","thread_id":""}]}
{"id":"taskflow-case-15-multi-orchestrator-ownership","display_id":null,"title":"CASE-15 multi orchestrator ownership","description":"Verify current owner, stale owner, and competing owner. Doctor/status must not give clean pass when ownership conflict is active.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"1779189396518208200","created_by":"vida task","updated_at":"1779313989368080600","closed_at":"1779313989368080600","close_reason":"case15-doctor-status-ownership-claim-conflict-surface-parity-fixed-and-rungraph-closure-complete","source_repo":"C:\\project\\vida-stack","compaction_level":0,"original_size":0,"notes":"# CASE-15 Audit Findings — Validation & Correction Required\n\n**Audit Date:** 2026-05-21  \n**Scope:** Multi orchestrator ownership, stale owner detection, competing owner handling  \n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Critical Defects (Need Fix Before Case Validation)\n\n### D1: Stale Sessions Never Purged From Disk\n**File:** `orchestrator_session_surface.rs:340-352`  \n`classify_sessions()` returns `(live_other, stale)` tuples and writes them back in order, but stale entries remain in sessions.json indefinitely. Over time this bloats the file. The `merge_current_session()` only prunes legacy synthesized tokens (line 218-224), not general stale sessions.\n\n**Impact:** sessions.json grows unbounded; stale session detection becomes slower and less accurate over time.\n\n### D3: Claim Expiry Minimum of 1 Second Is Dangerously Short\n**File:** `state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs:145-146`  \n```rust\nlet bounded_seconds = if lease_seconds == 0 { 1 } else { lease_seconds };\n```\nA caller passing `lease_seconds=0` gets a 1-second TTL. While this prevents division-by-zero, it means claims expire almost immediately unless the caller always passes explicit positive values.\n\n### D6: Heartbeat Does Not Check Owning Session Liveness\n**File:** `state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs:481-523`  \nAfter acquiring ClaimAcquireGuard and verifying resource_revision, only checks claim-level active/expired status. Never cross-references `orchestrator_session_id` against current session store to verify the claiming orchestrator's session hasn't gone stale or been superseded by another orchestrator.\n\n### D7: Foreign Claims With Expired Sessions Still Appear in Conflicts\n**File:** `operator_session_projection.rs:54-68`  \nA foreign claim with `lease_mode=exclusive` and an expired/stale owning session still appears in `claim_conflicts`. The projection should filter out claims whose owning orchestrator sessions are stale (no heartbeat within SESSION_TTL_SECONDS or dead process).\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Medium Defects (Should Fix Before Case Validation)\n\n### D2: Future Heartbeat Classified as Stale Without Grace Period\n**File:** `orchestrator_session_surface.rs:350`  \nLine 349: `heartbeat_fresh = heartbeat <= now && (now - heartbeat) <= SESSION_TTL_SECONDS`. A future heartbeat fails `heartbeat <= now`, so it goes straight to stale without a process liveness check. Clock-skewed orchestrator sessions immediately marked stale — no grace period for clock drift.\n\n### D4: Windows Path Normalization Lowercases, Linux/macOS Does Not\n**File:** `state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs:168-170`  \nWindows normalizes paths to lowercase; same path on Linux stays mixed-case and would fail intersection checks against claims from other platforms. Creates cross-platform inconsistency in ownership verification.\n\n### D5: Two Different Staleness Semantics Coexist Without Coordination\nOrchestrator sessions use `SESSION_TTL_SECONDS = 7200` (2 hours). Orchestrator claims use configurable `lease_seconds` (default 60s in tests). No unified staleness policy — a claim can expire long before its owning session is considered stale, or vice versa.\n\n### D9: Scheduler Reservations Have No Lease Mode\n**File:** `state_store_scheduler_reservation.rs:265-270`  \nUnlike orchestrator claims which support Exclusive/SharedRead/Observe modes, scheduler dispatch reservations only check task_id and conflict_domain collisions (lines 138-149). Two different launch roles for the same task cannot coexist even if they're disjoint operations.\n\n---\n\n## 🔵 Dead Code Identified\n\n### DC1: `local_process_liveness()` Always Returns Unknown on Unsupported Platforms\n**File:** `orchestrator_session_surface.rs:265-308`  \nFallback arm (line 294-297) for other platforms only checks PID equality and returns Unknown. Any orchestrator session from an unsupported platform is treated as live_other (never stale), potentially blocking mutations indefinitely.\n\n### DC3: Host Agents Write Guard Data Silently Dropped When host_agents Is None\n**File:** `status_surface_truth_inputs.rs:41-50`  \nIf `build_host_agent_status_summary()` returns None (e.g., no agents configured), the write guard data is silently dropped from host_agents object. Downstream consumers can't cross-reference agent state with write guard state in a single structure.\n\n### DC5: MAX_SESSION_STORE_BYTES = 1MB Never Enforced On Write\n**File:** `orchestrator_session_surface.rs:16-18`  \nRead path checks file size (line 104), but write path has no equivalent guard. A buggy caller could write sessions.json exceeding 1MB, which would then be silently ignored by all subsequent reads — creating inconsistent state where writes succeed but are never visible.\n\n---\n\n## 🔶 Inconsistencies\n\n### I1: Claim vs Session Staleness Coupling\nClaims expire based on `lease_seconds` (configurable per-claim), sessions use fixed 2-hour TTL. No mechanism to cascade session staleness into claim invalidation. A claim can outlive its owning session by hours, or expire minutes after acquisition.\n\n### I2: Global vs Scoped Conflict Checking\n`active_orchestrator_claims()` queries ALL active claims across all scopes (line 365-374), while `acquire_orchestrator_claim()` uses scoped variant `active_orchestrator_claims_for_scope()` (line 391-408). Cross-worktree claims appear as foreign_blockers even when in different scopes.\n\n### I3: Resource Revision vs File Lock Granularity\nBoth ClaimAcquireGuard and heartbeat use single global file lock (`.vida-orchestrator-claim-acquire.guard`), but claim operations are scoped to `state_root_id + worktree_environment_id`. Unnecessary contention when multiple worktrees operate independently.\n\n### I5: Blocker Code Propagation Gap\n`operator_session_projection.claim_conflicts` includes claim-level blocker_codes (line 68), but these are never surfaced to top-level operator contract. Claim-level blockers remain isolated in claim_conflicts array without escalation logic.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Search Criteria for Validation\n\n```bash\n# Ownership conflict detection\ngrep -rn \"claim_is_expired\\|lease_mode.*exclusive\" crates/vida/src/state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs | head -20\ngrep -rn \"active_orchestrator_claims_for_scope\" crates/vida/src/state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs\n\n# Session staleness\ngrep -rn \"classify_sessions_with_liveness\\|SESSION_TTL_SECONDS\" crates/vida/src/orchestrator_session_surface.rs | head -20\n\n# Claim expiration\ngrep -rn \"lease_expires_at.*<=.*now\\|claim_expiry\" crates/vida/src/state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs | head -20\n\n# Path normalization\ngrep -rn \"normalize_claim_path\\|path_normalize\\|to_ascii_lowercase\" crates/vida/src/state_store_orchestrator_claim.rs\n\n# Scheduler reservation modes\ngrep -rn \"lease_mode\\|SharedRead\\|Observe\" crates/vida/src/state_store_scheduler_reservation.rs | head -10\n```\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Recommended Fixes (Priority Order)\n\n1. **F1:** Cascade session staleness to claims — add `invalidate_claims_for_stale_session(session_id)` that marks all active claims for stale orchestrator as \"released\" with reason `\"session_stale_cascade\"`\n2. **F2:** Add claim-level session liveness check in `heartbeat_orchestrator_claim()` — cross-reference claim's `orchestrator_session_id` against current session store\n3. **F5:** Fix cross-platform path normalization — normalize all paths to lowercase regardless of platform, or add platform-independent canonical form\n4. **F7:** Escalate claim-level blockers to operator contracts — extract unique blocker_codes from all claim_conflicts and merge into global_blockers\n5. **F3:** Implement periodic stale session cleanup — remove sessions older than 2x SESSION_TTL_SECONDS during `build_runtime_owner_evidence()`\n6. **F4:** Add lease_mode to scheduler reservations (Exclusive/SharedRead/Observe) or document why they don't need them\n7. **F6:** Add write-size guard to sessions.json — MAX_SESSION_STORE_BYTES check before writing\n\n---\n\n**Validation Gate:** Before validating CASE-15, fix D1 (stale session bloat), D3 (1-second claim expiry), and D6 (heartbeat doesn't check owning session liveness). These are the root causes of potential false-positive ownership conflicts.\n","labels":["active-case-15","case-15","multi-orchestrator","ownership","ownership-claim-surface-parity","taskflow-testing"],"execution_semantics":{"execution_mode":"sequential","order_bucket":"adversarial-readiness","parallel_group":null,"conflict_domain":"taskflow-release-readiness"},"planner_metadata":{"owned_paths":["crates/vida/src/doctor_surface.rs","crates/vida/src/operator_session_projection.rs","crates/vida/src/status_surface.rs","crates/vida/src/status_surface_operator_contracts.rs","crates/vida/tests"],"acceptance_targets":[],"proof_targets":["cargo-test-doctor-claim-conflicts","cargo-test-status-operator-claim-conflicts","vida-doctor-json","vida-status-json"],"risk":null,"estimate":null,"lane_hint":null},"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"taskflow-case-15-multi-orchestrator-ownership","depends_on_id":"taskflow-case-14-stale-dispatch-and-recovery","edge_type":"blocks","created_at":"1779189506672421200","created_by":"vida","metadata":"{}","thread_id":""},{"issue_id":"taskflow-case-15-multi-orchestrator-ownership","depends_on_id":"taskflow-adversarial-autonomy-and-release-readiness-testing-epic","edge_type":"parent-child","created_at":"1